      "response": "\nBeach\n\nJohn Austin was painting furiously at his easel.\n\nFraulein Konstanza Von Goethe stood among the rocks, apparently in deep thought.\n\nSeeing me, Konstanza left her pose and approached me. John didn't leave his \neasel, but watched us from a distance. He seemed to be happy, for a change. \n\n\"Herr Dawson,\" said Konstanza, and her eyes were bright with a happiness I had \nnever seen in them before. \"I saw the boat at the pier, and I wanted to speak \nwith you before you left.\" \n\nI nodded. \"What is it?\" \n\nKonstanza dropped her voice to a whisper. \"I think I am in love, with Herr \nAustin. And more than that, I think he is in love with me too. And Papa ... \nHerr Dawson, I feel I owe all of this to you. When I first saw you, with your \ntrunk at the end of the pier, I never thought you would be something like a \nguardian angel to me.\" \n\nI blushed at this rather extravagant expression of quite undeserved gratitude, \nand tried to wave it off, but Konstanza stopped me. With a kiss, on the cheek, \nsuch as one might give to a favorite uncle. \n\nIt was enough. I smiled, made as dignified a farewell as I could to John and to \nKonstanza, and retreated. Almost in a dream, I retrieved my trunk and boarded \nthe boat. \n\nI wasn't sure what to think.\n\n[touch SPACE]\n",
